-- title:
--   Squared isogeny degree equals the relative index of kernel ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic a prime $q'$, algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/q'$, and let $X_1$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ all of whose $q'$-torsion points vanish. Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ satisfy $a<0$, $b<0$ and, for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_\mathbb{Q}$, the condition that $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_\mathbb{Q}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division ring exactly when $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda_1\subseteq\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule which is an order (contains $1$, is multiplicatively closed, spans over $\mathbb{Q}$, finitely generated) and is maximal among orders containing it, and let $\theta_1$ be an injective ring map from the subring of $\mathrm{End}$ of $\kappa$-points generated by the rational homomorphisms $X_1\to X_1$ onto $\Lambda_1$. For a finite idele $y$ write $I_y=\mathrm{ofFiniteIdele}\,\Lambda_1\,y$, the set of quaternions whose image in $\mathbb{H}\otimes_\mathbb{Q}\mathbb{A}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ lies in $y$ times the additive group generated by the $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in\Lambda_1$ and $\alpha$ an integral finite adele. Fix a finite idele $x$, an elliptic $W$ over $\kappa$, a nonzero rational homomorphism $\chi:X_1\to W$ and a unit $d\in\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]^\times$ such that $\theta_1$ carries the set of rational endomorphisms of $X_1$ of the form $\chi$ followed by a rational homomorphism $W\to X_1$ onto the elementwise conjugate of $d\cdot I_x$. Let $g$ be a finite idele whose underlying element lies in that integral box, let $\psi:W\to W'$ and $\psi':W'\to W$ be rational homomorphisms between elliptic curves with $\psi$ followed by $\psi'$ and $\psi'$ followed by $\psi$ both equal to multiplication by $n=\#\ker\psi$, and assume $\theta_1$ carries the set of rational endomorphisms of $X_1$ of the form $\chi$ followed by $\psi$ followed by a rational homomorphism $W'\to X_1$ onto the elementwise conjugate of $d\cdot I_{xg}$. Then $n^2$ equals the relative index of $I_{xg}$ in $I_x$ as additive groups, i.e. the index of $I_{xg}\cap I_x$ in $I_x$.
--
--   This is the quantitative half of the kernel-ideal dictionary of Deuring and Waterhouse for supersingular elliptic curves over an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q'$: the degree of the isogeny realising a change of ideal class matches, after squaring, the index of the corresponding right ideals of a maximal order in the definite quaternion algebra ramified exactly at $q'$. It feeds the Čerednik–Drinfel'd package, where it is used to identify the degree of the connecting isogeny with the level and to parametrise supersingular points by ideal classes.
